Subject: [PATCH 69/69] drivers/floppy.c: complete the x86 write over ISA DMA -- the sector reaches the host image and survives a power cycle; the non-DMA write could not complete against QEMU

Arch-tested: x86

THE WRITE IS NOW REAL. Patch 07 left the x86 floppy backing HALF done: the read
path worked, the write transferred all 512 bytes and then hung at MSR 0x30
(BUSY|NON_DMA) until a bounded poll gave up, failing honestly with WRITE FAILED.
This patch makes the write land. `setenv boot-file X; update-nvram` on floppy0
now returns success, the bytes appear in the host image, and a fresh boot on
that image reads X back -- the "persist" shape, on a third backing.

WHY THE NON-DMA WRITE COULD NOT COMPLETE (diagnosed 2026-10-03, TODO 24.1). It
is a QEMU model bug, not a driver one. For a single-sector non-DMA write
(EOT == start sector, MT cleared -- which patch 07 already did), hw/block/fdc.c's
fdctrl_write_data() runs `if (!fdctrl_seek_to_next_sect()) break;` BEFORE the
`if (data_pos == data_len) fdctrl_stop_transfer()`. seek_to_next_sect() returns
0 at end-of-track for a non-multi-track command, so the break fires,
stop_transfer is skipped, NON_DMA never clears, and the controller never turns
the bus around. The non-DMA READ path escapes only because it sets MT on a
double-sided disk, so its end-of-track seek returns 1. The DMA path has no such
hazard: fdctrl_transfer_handler() falls through to end_transfer:, which ALWAYS
calls fdctrl_stop_transfer. So DMA is not an optimisation here, it is the only
path QEMU completes.

THE BOUNCE, AND THE MISDIAGNOSIS IT CORRECTS. ISA DMA addresses only the low
16 MiB (a 24-bit address), but OpenBIOS x86 relocates itself to the TOP of RAM:
virt_to_phys() of a heap buffer measured 0x1ffcf910 (~512 MiB under -m 512), far
out of the 8237's reach. A 2026-10-03 prototype got DMA *completing* but wrote
zeros, and the finding recorded then blamed "the firmware's virt<->phys for
arbitrary low memory is not the simple linear alias phys_to_virt assumes (its
MMU/segment map is the open question)." THAT WAS WRONG, and the correction is the
heart of this patch. x86 here has NO paging; translation is a flat segment whose
base is virt_offset (arch/x86/segment.c), and a segment base wraps mod 2^32, so
a physical address round-trips through phys_to_virt() EXACTLY. Measured, not
reasoned: a sector staged at phys_to_virt(0x100000) read back byte-for-byte from
QEMU's monitor (`xp /8xb 0x100000`) -- an observer OUTSIDE the guest -- equal to
the source. The prototype's zeros were a bug in its 8237 programming (the page
register for a 1 MiB address), not in the memory map. So the fix stages each
sector in a fixed low page (FLOPPY_DMA_BOUNCE_PHYS = 0x100000): below 16 MiB,
inside one 64 KiB DMA page, and free in a floppy boot (the firmware runs from
the top; nothing is loaded at 1 MiB).

VERIFIED, with a control that bites. `./smoke-openbios.sh floppy`: the positive
arm writes a nonce via update-nvram and finds it in the HOST image; a fresh boot
on that image prints the nonce back from printenv (survives the power cycle);
the no-write control leaves the image blank. Two observers that the firmware
cannot fake -- the host file and QEMU's monitor.

X86 ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION. drivers/floppy.c is CONFIG_DRIVER_FLOPPY; among this
lab's arches only x86 enables it (amd64 and ppc build it out), and the code here
is ISA-8237-specific (raw outb to the DMA ports, a <16 MiB bounce). The write
path is reached only from arch/x86/openbios.c's nvram backing. tools/
check-patch-scope.sh carries the matching exemption with this reason.

Verified by: ./smoke-openbios.sh floppy

--- a/drivers/floppy.c
+++ b/drivers/floppy.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 #include "libc/vsprintf.h"
 
 #include "drivers/drivers.h"
+#include "asm/io.h"             /* phys_to_virt/virt_to_phys for the DMA bounce */
 
 #include "timer.h"
 
@@ -49,6 +50,33 @@
 #define DOR_MOT_EN2	0x40
 #define DOR_MOT_EN3	0x80
 
+/* ISA 8237 DMA controller 1 (the 8-bit channels), ports for CHANNEL 2 -- the
+ * channel the PC floppy controller has been wired to since the 5150. Writing a
+ * sector is a memory->device ("read") transfer: the 8237 reads the bounce
+ * buffer and streams it to the FDC. See floppy_write_sector for why DMA at all
+ * (QEMU cannot complete a single-sector NON-DMA write -- TODO 24.1). */
+#define DMA1_CH2_ADDR	0x04		/* channel 2 base/current address */
+#define DMA1_CH2_COUNT	0x05		/* channel 2 base/current count   */
+#define DMA1_CH2_PAGE	0x81		/* channel 2 page register (A16-23) */
+#define DMA1_MASK	0x0a		/* single-channel mask register   */
+#define DMA1_MODE	0x0b		/* mode register                  */
+#define DMA1_CLEAR_FF	0x0c		/* clear the byte-pointer flip-flop */
+#define DMA_MODE_SINGLE_READ_CH2  0x4a	/* single | read-from-memory | ch 2 */
+#define DMA_MASK_SET_CH2	  0x06	/* set   mask bit for channel 2   */
+#define DMA_MASK_CLEAR_CH2	  0x02	/* clear mask bit for channel 2   */
+
+/* A fixed low-memory bounce page for the floppy write DMA. ISA DMA addresses
+ * only the low 16 MiB (a 24-bit address), but OpenBIOS x86 relocates itself to
+ * the TOP of RAM, so virt_to_phys(a stack/heap buffer) is far out of the
+ * 8237's reach. This page is in free conventional-extended RAM (the firmware
+ * runs from the top; nothing is loaded at 1 MiB in a floppy boot) and, being
+ * below 16 MiB and inside a single 64 KiB DMA page, is a legal 8237 target.
+ * phys_to_virt() reaches it EXACTLY: x86 translation here is a flat segment
+ * whose base is virt_offset (arch/x86/segment.c), with no paging, so the
+ * segment base wraps mod 2^32 and a physical address round-trips -- measured
+ * with QEMU's monitor reading this page from outside the guest (TODO 24.1). */
+#define FLOPPY_DMA_BOUNCE_PHYS	0x100000UL
+
 /* Bits of main status register */
 #define STATUS_BUSYMASK	0x0F		/* drive busy mask */
 #define STATUS_BUSY	0x10		/* FDC busy */
@@ -1005,24 +1033,67 @@
 	return 1;
 }
 
-/* KNOWN-BLOCKED against QEMU's S82078B model, measured 2026-08-23.
+/* Program 8237 DMA controller 1, channel 2, for a memory->device transfer of
+ * `len` bytes starting at physical `phys`. `phys` must be below 16 MiB and must
+ * not cross a 64 KiB boundary (the 8237's address counter is 16 bits + an
+ * independent page latch that does NOT increment on carry). The channel is
+ * MASKED first so the controller never acts on a half-written descriptor, and
+ * UNMASKED last so the FDC's DRQ is serviced the instant the write starts. */
+static void floppy_dma_setup_write(unsigned long phys, unsigned len)
+{
+	unsigned count = len - 1;	/* the 8237 transfers count+1 bytes */
+
+	outb(DMA_MASK_SET_CH2, DMA1_MASK);		/* mask channel 2        */
+	outb(0, DMA1_CLEAR_FF);				/* reset byte-pointer FF */
+	outb(DMA_MODE_SINGLE_READ_CH2, DMA1_MODE);	/* single, mem->device   */
+	outb(0, DMA1_CLEAR_FF);
+	outb(phys & 0xff, DMA1_CH2_ADDR);		/* address A0-A7         */
+	outb((phys >> 8) & 0xff, DMA1_CH2_ADDR);	/* address A8-A15        */
+	outb((phys >> 16) & 0xff, DMA1_CH2_PAGE);	/* page    A16-A23       */
+	outb(0, DMA1_CLEAR_FF);
+	outb(count & 0xff, DMA1_CH2_COUNT);		/* count low             */
+	outb((count >> 8) & 0xff, DMA1_CH2_COUNT);	/* count high            */
+	outb(DMA_MASK_CLEAR_CH2, DMA1_MASK);		/* unmask channel 2      */
+}
+
+/* Write one 512-byte sector over ISA DMA (8237 channel 2), the way a real PC
+ * floppy driver does -- and the way this one MUST, because the CPU-fed non-DMA
+ * write cannot complete against QEMU.
  *
- * All 512 bytes transfer ("sent 512 of 512"), and then the controller stays at
- * status 0x30 (BUSY|NON_DMA) through 200,000 polls: it never turns the bus
- * around, so result() reads no status bytes and reports -1. The bytes go out
- * and the completion never comes back. The READ path on the same controller
- * works (see the ST0_HA fix in read_ok), so this is specific to the non-DMA
- * write turnaround, not to the driver's command plumbing in general.
+ * WHY NOT NON-DMA. Measured 2026-08-23, diagnosed 2026-10-03 (TODO 24.1): for a
+ * single-sector non-DMA write QEMU's hw/block/fdc.c runs
+ * `if (!fdctrl_seek_to_next_sect()) break;` BEFORE `if (data_pos==data_len)
+ * fdctrl_stop_transfer()`. With EOT==start and MT clear, seek_to_next returns 0
+ * at end-of-track on a non-multi-track command, so stop_transfer is skipped,
+ * NON_DMA never clears, and MSR sticks at 0x30 forever. The non-DMA READ path
+ * escapes this only because it sets MT (double-sided), so its end-of-track seek
+ * returns 1. The DMA path has no such hazard: QEMU's fdctrl_transfer_handler
+ * falls through to end_transfer:, which ALWAYS calls fdctrl_stop_transfer, so
+ * the controller turns the bus around and the result phase arrives.
  *
- * It fails HONESTLY -- bounded waits, a named message, and arch_nvram_put
- * reporting WRITE FAILED -- which is the point. An earlier draft hung here
